# Trivy ignore list — justify EVERY entry
# Format: <CVE-ID>
# Each entry MUST have a comment line above explaining justification + expiry.
#
# This file can ONLY suppress by vulnerability ID. To suppress a whole package,
# do not paste its CVE list here — it goes stale as soon as a new one is
# published. Add a rule to .trivy/ignore-policy.rego instead, which matches on
# the package. (linux-libc-dev is already handled there.)

# Example:
# # justification: false positive — affects only debian buster, we run bookworm. expires 2026-12-31
# CVE-2024-99999

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Go stdlib compiled into the static docker CLI (tenant-orchestrator image)
#
# justification: these are go1.26.5 stdlib CVEs baked into
# /usr/local/bin/docker, the static Docker CLI the orchestrator uses to run
# `docker exec odoo-mgmt odoo -d <db> --init=...`. They cannot be fixed by us
# today: the newest published static release, 29.7.2, is still built with
# go1.26.5 (verified by `go version` on the extracted binary), and the fixes
# land in go1.26.6. Bumping the pin changes nothing.
#
# Why the exposure is not real here: the binary is a local CLI. It serves no
# HTTP, terminates no TLS, and parses no attacker-supplied ASN.1, XML, URLs or
# templates — it opens /var/run/docker.sock, which is mounted read-only, and
# exits. Every finding below is a DoS or content-injection reachable only by
# feeding untrusted input to a server, which this binary never is.
#
# These are listed by ID rather than as a package rule on purpose. A rule on
# `stdlib` would silence every future Go CVE in every Go binary we ship, and the
# whole point is that the NEXT batch should fail loudly.
#
# On 26-Aug this binary scanned clean; these eight were published since. Recheck
# when Docker ships a static build on go1.26.6 or later, drop this block, and
# re-scan. expires 2026-10-01
CVE-2026-33818
CVE-2026-39821
CVE-2026-46600
CVE-2026-56853
CVE-2026-56858
CVE-2026-56859
CVE-2026-56860
CVE-2026-56862
